From 1d2ace86084e4077656150470cf8e59281068a6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_sched_tc_fixed <>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:27:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] apps/examples/testcase: complete the round-robin and SMP
 scenario families

Adds the six remaining catalogued scenarios, closing out families A and H.

The harness gains per worker scheduling policy, so one workload can mix
SCHED_FIFO and SCHED_RR tasks, and a switch detection mode in which each
worker notices when control has been away and come back.  Summed across
workers that counts how many times the scheduler moved the CPU between them,
which is what makes a rotation rate measurable from application code at all.

RR-04 puts a SCHED_FIFO task and a SCHED_RR task at equal priority on one
CPU, with the FIFO task created first so it owns the CPU when the other
appears.  A scheduler that rotates on timeslice expiry without consulting
the policy passes every other scenario in the family and fails here.

RR-05 runs an oversubscribed workload alongside continuous scheduler lock
churn and checks that every worker still gets a share.  It targets the
timeslice refill defect: a slice that expires while the scheduler is locked
and is refilled rather than left expired lets the running task earn a fresh
full slice every time, so with the lock taken continuously it never rotates.
The distinction from the throughput scenario in the inversion family is
deliberate, that one asks whether the system still does work and this asks
whether the work is still shared.

RR-06 measures how often the CPU actually changes hands and compares it
against the window divided by CONFIG_RR_INTERVAL.  Every other scenario asks
whether rotation happens; this asks how often, which catches a timeslice
decremented once per CPU per tick rather than once per tick.  That defect
still rotates, and still shares fairly, just at a multiple of the configured
rate.

SMP-02 takes a CPU offline and brings it back under load, checking that no
task is lost or stranded on a CPU that is no longer there.  SMP-03 asks the
narrower question of whether timeslicing still behaves afterwards, since a
CPU returning with stale accounting would keep every task alive and still
leave the shares permanently skewed.  SMP-04 samples which task occupies
each CPU by walking the task lists, because a CPU left idle while work is
queued is invisible to per task counters: the tasks that did run look fine
and the ones that did not simply look slow.

SMP-03 is narrower than the catalogue states, and the source records why.
The catalogue frames it around a power management sleep and wake cycle;
only the part the scheduler owns is covered, namely taking the secondary
CPUs down and back, which is what the power management path itself does
first.  Driving a real sleep would require the board to actually sleep,
which a test cannot do unattended, so tick compensation across a sleep
remains uncovered.

Both the SMP and non-SMP compilation paths are warning free under -Wall
-Wextra.
